Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.50.5.18", "inferred_role": "internal_compute_production_server_compromised", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:08:42+0200", "last_seen": "2028-05-17T00:14:42+0200", "agent_name": "COMPUTE-PROD-07.corp.local"},
    {"ip": "185.220.101.91", "inferred_role": "external_monero_mining_pool_front", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:14:02+0200", "last_seen": "2028-05-17T00:14:42+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Web Server Compromise (webshell pivot)", "technique": "T1190 + T1505.003", "what_happened": "At 2028-05-17 00:08:42 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on COMPUTE-PROD-07.corp.local (10.50.5.18): powershell.exe launched with the suspicious '-nop -enc <base64>' pattern, parentImage=w3wp.exe (IIS worker process), user=IIS APPPOOL\\WebApp. The w3wp.exe parent is the universal webshell-signature: a legitimate IIS worker spawning a script interpreter under the app pool identity means the web application is executing attacker-supplied code (web.config tamper or an .aspx webshell in wwwroot). Decoded base64 reveals a download cradle pulling a remote payload.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=powershell.exe + commandLine contains '-nop -enc' + parentImage=w3wp.exe + user=IIS APPPOOL\\WebApp. The (parent=w3wp.exe + child=powershell.exe + encoded payload) triple is a near-deterministic webshell signature — IIS workers do not legitimately spawn encoded PowerShell.", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:08:42+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Execution / Ingress Tool Transfer (binary drop)", "technique": "T1105", "what_happened": "At 00:09:18, Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) on COMPUTE-PROD-07: targetFilename=C:\\Windows\\Temp\\winserv.exe, signed=false, hash=a8d4c2b1f9e7c8d3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1a2b3c4d5e6f7. The hash matches a known xmrig (Monero miner) family — public TI / Sigma communities track this hash. The filename 'winserv.exe' is masquerading-style naming intended to read as a legitimate Windows service binary; the actual location (C:\\Windows\\Temp, an unprivileged writable directory) and the unsigned flag give it away.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11: targetFilename=C:\\Windows\\Temp\\winserv.exe + signed=false + hash on the known-xmrig family list. Time-correlation: 36 seconds after the encoded-powershell at step 1, consistent with download cradle → disk drop.", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:09:18+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Resource Hijacking (miner launch)", "technique": "T1496", "what_happened": "At 00:09:42, Sysmon Event 1 on COMPUTE-PROD-07: image=C:\\Windows\\Temp\\winserv.exe + commandLine='winserv.exe -o xmr.pool.example:3333 -u 4AbC...XMRwallet -t 8' + parentImage=powershell.exe + user=IIS APPPOOL\\WebApp. The '-o <host>:<port>' / '-u <wallet>' / '-t <threads>' argument shape is the xmrig CLI signature — '-o' specifies the mining pool URL, '-u' specifies the attacker's Monero wallet (4AbC... is a Monero address prefix), '-t 8' allocates 8 CPU threads for hashing. The miner is now running under the webshell-driven app pool identity, not SYSTEM, so it lacks privilege escalation YET — but the resource theft is already underway.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image ending in winserv.exe + commandLine contains '-o ' AND ':3333' AND '-u ' (xmrig CLI flag signature) + parentImage=powershell.exe + user=IIS APPPOOL\\WebApp. The 24-second gap from binary drop to execution = scripted, not interactive.", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:09:42+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Impact (sustained CPU exhaustion)", "technique": "T1496", "what_happened": "At 00:12:45, Wazuh CPU-metric decoder emitted a sustained-utilization alert on COMPUTE-PROD-07: CPU utilization reached 98% sustained for a 6-hour rolling window observation against this host's baseline of ~12% for its workload profile (compute production server with steady but not full-throttle load). The 8.2× baseline deviation is well above the anomaly threshold. Sustained 98% CPU on a production compute host means: (a) legitimate workloads are starved or throttled, (b) electrical / cooling / cloud-billing cost spikes, (c) accelerated hardware aging, (d) high-fidelity indicator of cryptomining when paired with Stratum-port outbound traffic (step 5).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh CPU-metric decoder rule on COMPUTE-PROD-07: utilization >= 90% sustained over 5+ min window AND baseline-deviation > 5×. The CPU signal in isolation is ambiguous (could be legitimate batch job), but combined with steps 1-3 + step 5 (Stratum outbound) it is diagnostic of miner execution.", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:12:45+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Command-and-Control (Stratum mining protocol egress)", "technique": "T1071", "what_happened": "At 00:14:02, Sysmon Event 3 (NetworkConnect) on COMPUTE-PROD-07: image=C:\\Windows\\Temp\\winserv.exe + DestinationIp=185.220.101.91 + DestinationPort=3333 + Protocol=tcp. Port 3333 (alongside 3334) is the industry-standard Stratum mining protocol port — Stratum is the JSON-RPC over TCP protocol that miners use to receive work units from the pool and submit shares. The destination 185.220.101.91 (NL Hetzner) is on the public-TI mining-pool-front list. Persistent TCP connection (long-lived, not short-burst) = active mining session, not one-off contact.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: image=*\\winserv.exe + destinationPort=3333 + destinationIp=185.220.101.91 (public, not RFC1918). The (Stratum-port 3333/3334) signal is high-fidelity when the source process is unsigned + in C:\\Windows\\Temp.", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:14:02+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Command-and-Control (DNS resolution of pool domain)", "technique": "T1071.004", "what_happened": "At 00:14:18, Sysmon Event 22 (DnsQuery) on COMPUTE-PROD-07: queryName=xmr.pool.example, queryResult resolves to 185.220.101.91. The 'xmr.pool.*' / 'pool.minexmr.*' / 'xmr-pool.*' / etc. naming families are tracked by mining-pool domain lists. DNS-resolution evidence confirms the miner is performing pool-lookup, not just hitting a hard-coded IP — typical of distributed mining-pool infrastructure where the pool front-end IP rotates.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 22: queryName=xmr.pool.example. Mining pool domains have predictable naming patterns and appear on public TI lists.", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:14:18+0200"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Persistence (scheduled task — restart on logon)", "technique": "T1053.005", "what_happened": "At 00:14:42, Sysmon Event 1 on COMPUTE-PROD-07: image=schtasks.exe + commandLine='schtasks.exe /create /tn \"WinServUpdate\" /tr \"C:\\Windows\\Temp\\winserv.exe -o xmr.pool.example:3333 -u 4AbC...\" /sc onlogon /ru SYSTEM /f' + parentImage=cmd.exe + user=IIS APPPOOL\\WebApp. The /tn \"WinServUpdate\" name is masquerading-style (sounds like a Windows update task). /sc onlogon ensures the miner restarts whenever any user logs on. /ru SYSTEM elevates the next execution to SYSTEM privilege — escalation lever from the current app-pool identity. /f forces overwrite without prompt. Windows Security Event 4698 (scheduled-task-created) will mirror this event.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=schtasks.exe + commandLine contains '/create' + '/tn' + '/tr' pointing to winserv.exe + '/ru SYSTEM' = persistence + privilege-escalation primitive. Reinforced by Windows Security Event 4698 on the same host at the same timestamp.", "first_seen": "2028-05-17T00:14:42+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2028-05-17T00:08:42+0200", "end": "2028-05-17T00:14:42+0200"},
